/* On a phone the strip would push the table off the first screen, so it drops
   to the two readings that answer \"is the market working right now\" and the
   per-metric paths go with them.

   It also STACKS: side by side, the stamp sat alone against a two-row column of
   metrics, wasting the width it was holding and reading like an accident. Stamp
   on its own line, metrics sharing the full width beneath it. */
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  ._strip_cctjd_9 {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 7px;
    padding: 8px 10px;
  }
  ._items_cctjd_75 {
    width: 100%;
    /* NOT space-between: with two readings it puts the whitespace BETWEEN them,
       which splits one group into two disconnected clusters. Grouped left, the
       pair reads as a single line and the slack falls harmlessly at the end. */
    /* 12px, not the 18px this started at, because 18 WRAPPED at 375px once the
       values reached their widest — see the svg width note below. 12 clears
       that case with 4px spare and still reads as grouping: the gap INSIDE an
       item is 5px, so 12 keeps the ~2.4x inter/intra ratio that tells the eye
       where one reading ends (desktop runs 22 vs 8). */
    gap: 12px;
    justify-content: flex-start;
  }
  ._item_cctjd_75 { gap: 5px; }
  ._item_cctjd_75:nth-child(n + 3) { display: none; }
  ._since_cctjd_135 { display: none; }
  /* Each reading needs ~190px at the desktop path width, so two of them wrapped
     to a second row and the \"tidier\" stack came out TALLER than what it
     replaced. DayPath carries a viewBox, so CSS can scale it — shrink the path
     and both readings share one line. */
  /* 40px is a MEASURED CEILING, not a guess — do not widen it.
     With a full session's path the line looks cramped at 390px and there
     appears to be slack at the right, which invites exactly that change. That
     slack is an illusion of two things: the widest phone in this tier, and
     today's values. Measured at 375px (iPhone SE/mini) with the two
     phone-visible readings at their widest (`100.0` and `100.0%`), the row
     fits on ONE line only at 40px (15px spare) and 42px (11px). At 44px it
     wraps and the strip jumps 61px -> 92px. So the reachable gain is ~5%, and
     spending it would leave a phone-and-value combination that wraps in
     production with no CSS change to blame.
     That same measurement found a LATENT wrap that shipped on 2026-08-05: at
     the original 18px item gap, 375px + `100.0`/`100.0%` already wrapped. It
     had never been seen because it needs the narrowest phone AND values a
     character wider than a normal session prints. The gap above is now 12px,
     which is what actually fixes it — the 40px here is unchanged. */
  ._item_cctjd_75 svg { width: 40px; }
  ._label_cctjd_107 { font-size: var(--text-xs); letter-spacing: 0.6px; }
  ._value_cctjd_121 { font-size: var(--text-md); }
}

